---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import HostingChoice from '../../components/HostingChoice.astro';
import { RAILWAY_MONTHLY } from '../../lib/config';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Base title="Railway guide · Big Dipper" description="Step by step: set up, claim, update and back up your Little Dipper on Railway.">
  <article class="narrow prose" style="padding-top: 40px">
    <div class="kicker">Guide</div>
    <h1>Little Dipper on Railway</h1>
    <p class="lede">Everything from signing up to your first connection, and what to do when something doesn’t work. The short version is on <a href={`${base}/get-started`}>Get your Little Dipper</a>.</p>

    <HostingChoice />

    <h2>1. Sign up for Railway</h2>
    <p>Railway is the hosting company that runs your Little Dipper. Your Little Dipper costs {RAILWAY_MONTHLY} a month, paid to Railway with your own card, and the server lives in your account.</p>

    <h2>2. Set up your Little Dipper</h2>
    <p>Use the <strong>Deploy on Railway</strong> button on <a href={`${base}/get-started`}>Get your Little Dipper</a>. Railway fills in everything Little Dipper needs, including two secrets made just for you:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>LD_CLAIM_TOKEN</code>: your claim code. It proves you’re the one who set this server up.</li>
      <li><code>LD_KEYSTORE_KEY</code>: protects your Little Dipper’s keys. Never share it or change it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Wait until Railway shows the deployment as active, about two minutes.</p>

    <h2>3. Open it</h2>
    <p>Click your Little Dipper’s link in Railway. It ends in <code>.up.railway.app</code>, and it’s on your Little Dipper’s box in the project (also under <strong>Settings → Networking</strong>). Bookmark it: it’s where you’ll use your Little Dipper from now on.</p>

    <h2>4. Claim it</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Your Little Dipper says it’s waiting for its owner. Click <strong>Finish setting up</strong>.</li>
      <li>Big Dipper shows your Little Dipper’s address. Check it matches Railway’s, then continue. Big Dipper kept your claim code in this browser when you clicked Deploy, so your Little Dipper knows it’s you.</li>
      <li>Set up from a different browser or device? In Railway, open <strong>Variables</strong>, reveal <code>LD_CLAIM_TOKEN</code>, copy it, and paste it into your Little Dipper instead. The code works until you claim, so there’s no rush.</li>
      <li>Enter your name and email, then <strong>create a passkey</strong>: you’ll sign in with your face, fingerprint, device PIN or a security key. No password to forget.</li>
      <li><strong>Save your recovery codes</strong> somewhere safe, like a password manager. They’re how you get in if you lose your device.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>5. Save your recovery kit</h2>
    <p>Little Dipper asks for this on its Home page. The kit, with a passphrase you choose, lets you rebuild your Little Dipper on a new server if this one is ever lost. Keep the passphrase separate from the file. Nobody can reset it.</p>

    <h2>6. Updates</h2>
    <p>Railway installs new Little Dipper versions for you, overnight, after backing up your data. You can skip a version in Railway if you’d rather wait.</p>

    <h2>7. Keep a copy at home</h2>
    <p>Little Dipper reminds you each week to save a backup to your computer, or saves it automatically to a folder you choose (Chrome and Edge). With a backup and your recovery kit, you can move to another host or a device at home whenever you like.</p>
    <p><strong>Before you stop paying Railway</strong>, download a backup. When Railway shuts the server down, its data goes with it.</p>

    <h2>8. Connect with someone</h2>
    <p>In Little Dipper, open <strong>Contacts → Add</strong> and send an invite link to a friend who has a Little Dipper. You choose exactly what each person can see, and can change it any time.</p>

    <h2>When something doesn’t work</h2>
    <h3>Finish setting up says this browser doesn’t have your claim code</h3>
    <p>You clicked Deploy in a different browser or device. Finish there, or paste the code from Railway → Variables → <code>LD_CLAIM_TOKEN</code> into your Little Dipper.</p>
    <h3>The claim code isn’t accepted</h3>
    <p>Copy it again from Railway → Variables, without spaces. If you claimed already, sign in instead: the code stops working once your Little Dipper has an owner.</p>
    <h3>The address doesn’t load</h3>
    <p>Check the deployment is active in Railway, and that the address matches Settings → Networking exactly.</p>
    <h3>It keeps restarting</h3>
    <p>Open the deployment’s <strong>Logs</strong> in Railway. Make sure the volume is attached at <code>/data</code> and <code>LD_KEYSTORE_KEY</code> hasn’t been changed. Still stuck? Ask the person who invited you, and include the last lines of the log.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
